you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  as global temperatures tick up but  scientists warn a key  limit could be reached in the next five  years  the gulf state reliant on oil but at  risk from climate change  we speak to bahrain's climate envoy  and plea to leave nothing but footprints  behind  if you're visiting the uk's beaches this  half turn  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those developing the solutions  coming up the species of giant tortoise  rediscovered on the galapagos islands  after 100 years  but first let's go straight over to our  data dashboard and take a look at  this number here now this is an estimate  of how much  humans have warmed our planet since the  industrial revolution and you can see  it's ticking up every single second  now the aim agreed by 196 parties in the  paris accord of 2015  is to keep that number below 2 degrees  preferably  to 1.5 degrees of warming because above  that  we could see more extreme weather higher  sea levels  more people forced from their homes and  more climate-related disasters  but scientists are warning today there's  a 40 chance that will temporarily  hit 1.5 degrees in at least one of the  next  five years and these odds are increasing  all the time  according to the world meteorological  organization  well with me now is max dilly who's  climate program director at the world  meteorological  organization hello to you how much of a  concern is it if the temperature does go  up to that 1.5 degree  level temporarily if it then drops back  down again  yeah uh it's a good question because the  paris agreement obviously has a  long-term  focus and the the temperature goal in  the paris agreement is a long-term  global goal so if the temperature goes  above 1.5 degrees  as a global average temporarily it  doesn't mean that the paris agreement  has failed  uh intention is to try to keep that  long-term  uh temperature average within those  limits so  hopefully less than 1.5 degrees but  certainly less than two  the issue is and the the warning that  we're getting now  is that uh currently the global  temperature in 2020 was already 1.2  degrees above pre-industrial levels so  that's  close to 1.5 and now as you said in the  introduction  our forecasts are that there's a growing  chance that it could start to come up  above 1.5  um in the next five years well yes and  what are you learning about those longer  term averages to what extent  is the trend an upward one  well the trend is clearly an upward one  and we see that just  from the observational record  the warmest year on record was 2016.  that was about 1.3 degrees above  pre-industrial levels but there was a  strong el nino event at the time and  that tends to  give the temperature a little bit of a  boost but the last  five years have been the warmest five  years on record the last decade was the  warmest  decade on record and the decades  successively before  the last the current decade have all  been uh the warmest decades on record  to that point so we see a clear upward  trend and unless  uh something is done to control the  emissions that that trend will certainly  continue  and how do you do your calculations  well um the the the product uh that  has the uh current information that  we're discussing  now in it is a new product it was  developed  under the leadership of the the the met  office  um acting as a lead center for um  annual to decadal forecasting meaning  that the  the lead center pulls together forecast  information from the other  global producing centers of these  long-range forecasts  so to qualify as a forecast or a  prediction  there has to be a skill assessment it's  not a projection which is just  running the science forward and seeing  what the computer models suggest would  happen  it's actually something that's verified  by running it against historical data  and seeing what the skill is  the skill for global average temperature  is  a very highly skillful prediction so  because it's a it's a single number it's  not nearly as complicated to predict as  something like local rainfall which is  um something that's a lot more  challenging max delhi thanks very much  indeed  it's a pleasure in today's other climate  news the government's being urged to put  plastic pollution on the agenda when it  hosts next month's g7 summit  more than 30 business leaders  environmentalists and mps  want ministers to discuss a global  treaty on plastics  they say 300 million tons of plastic  waste is produced every year  while less than 10 of all plastic ever  produced has been recycled  co2 emissions from private jets have  increased by nearly a third  rising faster than commercial planes a  new report by  transport and environment shows carbon  dioxide from private flights in europe  increased by 31 between 2005  and 2019. the campaign group says the uk  is responsible for nearly a fifth of  these emissions making it the worst  country in europe  for private jet pollution  a tortoise thought to have become  extinct a hundred years ago has been  found  alive on the galapagos islands  scientists use genetic testing to  confirm the female is part of a species  known as the fernandina  giant tortoise an expedition is now  being planned to try and find  other surviving members of the species  an australian court has ruled the  government has a duty to protect  young people from the climate crisis  eight teenagers brought the case to  court to stop the expansion of a coal  mine in new south wales  the judge stopped short of issuing an  injunction saying he was satisfied the  environment minister  wouldn't breach her duty of care one of  the teenagers involved  anjali sharma said she sees today's  result as a win  the court has accepted the biggest part  of our  argument which was that the environment  owes us a duty of care  and now while the court today didn't  decide to grant an injunction  we have been granted an order um both  parties to come together and work out  um a way forward out of this so while it  might might seem like we've won the  battle but not the war the war is still  um still going on and the last part of  this battle is still up for grabs  and you can hear more from anjali in  this week's episode of climate cast is  out tomorrow  wherever you normally get your podcast  from  climate change impacts every single  country in the world but often we focus  on the role of the big emitters such as  china and the united states  but smaller nations such as bahrain have  a role to play too  and in a moment i'll be speaking to the  country's special envoy for climate  affairs  but first let's take a closer look at  the gulf state  well bahrain's position as a small  island means it's impacted by climate  change  in a variety of ways firstly rising sea  levels the highest point is 134 metres  above sea level  with most of the coast no higher than 5  meters and a mild  estimate on sea level rise due to  climate change predicts  more than a quarter of the country could  be underwater by the end of the century  now an increase in global temperature  will also exacerbate  the scarcity of usable water placing  bahrain  in the top seven countries water  stressed by 2040.  the country's economy relies on vast  sums generated from  oil refineries but the government has  applied for almost 10 million dollars of  assistance from the un's  green climate fund the country's carbon  dioxide emissions have been stable for  the last few years at around  22 tonnes of carbon dioxide per capita  now that puts it  in the top 10 of all countries in the  world in terms of co2  emissions per person well joining me now  is dr mohammed bin  dana special envoy of climate change for  the government of bahrain hello to you  so as we heard that bahrain is at risk  of suffering some of the worst effects  of climate change so how concerned are  you and what are you doing to mitigate  against the effects  we're actually thinking as a small  island developing states on on the  adaptations with the climate change  we managed to look at the sea level rise  we developed  a simulator that measures the impact of  the sea level rise on our shores  and our own infrastructures we set up a  target  220 50 of 0.5 meter  and we build a brazilian's plan to adapt  with the climate change  up to this level we managed also to work  on developing a national adaptation  investments plan because the financing  for the adaptation  project is is very important and crucial  now international  climate activism activism has played a  huge role  in recent weeks putting pressures on  governments and corporations  to meet their commitments on climate  change bahrain has  repeatedly stifled protest and activism  so can you guarantee that climate  activists  will be given freedom to speak out  we and bahrain have a very democratic  institution and we have the house of  parliament  and under um the government  the freedom of speech is always  available for  everybody in bahrain not just um  the environmentalist  human rights groups would argue with  that of course they would say that their  freedom of speech is stifled  they could argue as as much as they want  but this is not actually the truth the  truth is  everybody as per the constitution has  the right to speak in bahrain  okay well we will leave it there dr  muhammad bin dana thanks very much  indeed  thank you thank you now with holidays  abroad off the cards for many this  summer british beaches are expected to  be busier than ever  and a new campaign is hoping to keep our  coastline clean by asking everyone who  visits a beach  to pick up five pieces of litter well  earlier i spoke to tv presenter and un  patron of the wilderness  ben fogle and asked him why keeping  beaches clean  is so important you can see i'm on a  beautiful  [Music]  do have plenty of beach beaches like  this you only need to turn the corner  and find where the prevailing winds are  the prevailing currents and it's a very  different picture  there's a misconception that it's  picnickers who go to the beach and just  leave their rubbish behind that's not  really the case  it's stuff that has emptied into the  ocean rivers all around the world  it's um it's stuff that's been lost or  thrown off the back of ships and vessels  and i think we all have a  responsibility to protect  well that's everything from us for today  on the daily climate show tomorrow  how abandoned fishing nets littering the  ocean  are being transformed into ppe in  thailand  that's at the same time tomorrow here on  sky news see you then

